\begin{abstract}
The widespread digitisation of legal, academic, medical, and corporate records has intensified demand for verification systems that are simultaneously tamper-evident and privacy-preserving. Existing centralised approaches introduce single points of failure and expose document content to unnecessary third-party risk. This paper presents the Blockchain Document Verification System (BDVS), a web-based architecture that anchors document integrity proofs on the Ethereum Sepolia testnet while maintaining confidentiality through client-side encryption and off-chain ciphertext storage on the InterPlanetary File System (IPFS). Only the \texttt{keccak256} digest of each document is written to a Solidity smart contract. Encryption is performed entirely in the browser using an authenticated symmetric cipher (AES-256-GCM), with a per-wallet key derived from a signed wallet challenge; the backend server acts as a content-blind relay that handles ciphertext but never possesses the decryption key, plaintext, or material sufficient to reconstruct either. Request authentication is bound to wallet ownership through a signed, timestamped challenge verified server-side within a bounded replay window. The selection of \texttt{keccak256} over SHA-256, SHA-3, Blake2b, and MD5 is justified through a comparative evaluation of EVM execution cost, collision resistance, and protocol consistency. Empirical measurements indicate mean upload latency of 15--45 s (blockchain-confirmation-dominant) and mean download latency of 1--5 s (decentralised-storage-gateway-dominant). In a separate 1 MB microbenchmark, authenticated encryption added 1.17 ms relative to direct hashing. Initial document registration consumed approximately 208,000 gas on the local test network. A two-stage access-control model---server-side wallet authentication followed by smart-contract authorisation---combined with authenticated-cipher tag verification and per-download \texttt{keccak256} re-verification provides layered protection against unauthorised retrieval and ciphertext tampering without exposing plaintext to the backend.
\end{abstract}

