\section{Introduction}

Document authenticity verification is a foundational requirement across legal, academic, medical, and enterprise domains. Conventional pipelines delegate trust to centralised intermediaries---notaries, certificate authorities, or institutional registries---which introduces systemic vulnerabilities: single points of failure, elevated operational costs, exposure to insider fraud, and limited external auditability \cite{nakamoto2008bitcoin}.

The immutability property of distributed ledger technology offers a structurally different trust model. Once a transaction is incorporated into a confirmed block, the data it carries becomes computationally infeasible to alter without invalidating every subsequent block in the chain \cite{buterin2014ethereum}. Applied to document verification, this property yields a permanent, publicly auditable proof of registration. However, writing raw document content on-chain is neither economically viable nor architecturally appropriate. Under Ethereum's storage-gas schedule, changing a previously zero storage slot to a non-zero value incurs a base \texttt{SSTORE} cost of 20,000 gas, excluding cold-access and transaction overhead \cite{wood2022ethereum}; storing even modest documents would therefore be prohibitively expensive. Furthermore, the inherent transparency of public blockchains is incompatible with the confidentiality requirements of most document verification use cases.

BDVS resolves this tension through a deliberate separation of \textit{proof} from \textit{content}. A 256-bit \texttt{keccak256} digest---which changes irrevocably upon modification of even a single byte of the source document---is stored on-chain as an immutable registration record. The plaintext document is never exposed beyond the client browser: encryption is performed locally before any byte of file content leaves the user's machine, and the resulting ciphertext is pinned to a decentralised storage layer. The backend server acts purely as a content-blind relay that transports and stores ciphertext but never plaintext, keys, or initialisation vectors.

The system's design is grounded in three architectural commitments: client-side key derivation and encryption, an authenticated cipher mode, and signature-based request authorisation, so that the backend structurally cannot observe document content, has no way to forge a user's identity, and cannot serve tampered ciphertext without detection.

This paper makes the following contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item A complete reference architecture for privacy-preserving blockchain document verification, integrating an on-chain registry, a content-blind relay backend, decentralised ciphertext storage, and a client application performing all cryptographic operations locally.
\item A client-side, wallet-derived key-management scheme in which encryption keys are generated from a signed wallet challenge and a wallet-specific salt, so that the backend never has access to plaintext or key material.
\item A signed-challenge authorisation scheme with a replay window, providing cryptographic proof of wallet ownership on every authenticated request.
\item An empirical comparative analysis of \texttt{keccak256} against MD5, SHA-256, SHA-3 (NIST), and Blake2b, with quantitative justification in terms of EVM gas cost, collision resistance, and protocol consistency.
\item A two-stage access-control model combining server-side wallet authentication with smart-contract-level permission checks.
\item A per-download cryptographic integrity re-verification mechanism, reinforced at the cipher level by authenticated-encryption tags and at the application level by on-chain \texttt{keccak256} re-verification, that detects deviations from the originally registered document.
\end{enumerate}

A reference implementation, including the smart contract, backend relay, and client application described in this paper, is publicly available at the project's version-control repository \cite{bdvsrepo}; Section~\ref{sec:simenv} lists the specific technologies used to build and evaluate it.

\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Blockchain-Based Document Integrity}
Hasan and Salah \cite{hasan2018blockchain} demonstrated proof-of-existence using document hashes on a private Ethereum network, but did not address file privacy, key custody, or access control. Brotsis et al.\ \cite{brotsis2019blockchain} extended this model to digital forensic evidence by combining decentralised storage with Ethereum smart contracts; however, storage identifiers were kept on-chain, exposing file location metadata to all network participants. Nizamuddin et al.\ \cite{nizamuddin2019decentralized} adapted the model for academic certificate management, improving verifiability but providing no client-side or server-side key custody mechanism beyond ad hoc handling. BDVS addresses all three deficiencies: storage identifiers are never exposed on-chain, decryption keys are derived and used exclusively client-side so that no party other than the wallet owner ever possesses them, and fine-grained per-wallet access control is enforced at both the contract and API layers.

\subsection{Decentralised Storage}
Content-addressed, peer-to-peer storage protocols \cite{benet2014ipfs} are well suited to off-chain document storage. Several works have combined decentralised storage with blockchain-based integrity anchors \cite{chen2019blockchain, peng2024efficient}; the majority either retain plaintext content in storage---exposing files to any node---or store decryption keys on-chain, negating confidentiality. Naik et al.\ \cite{naik2024decentralized} employed symmetric encryption over a decentralised storage layer for file management but centralised key storage without a second authorisation tier. BDVS addresses both weaknesses: files are encrypted client-side, prior to ever leaving the browser, and decryption keys are never transmitted on-chain or to any server---eliminating the centralised key-storage bottleneck that Naik et al.\ leave unaddressed.

\subsection{Smart-Contract Access Control}
Attribute-based and role-based access control (RBAC) schemes on Ethereum have been proposed for IoT \cite{zhang2017iot} and healthcare \cite{azaria2016medrec}. Kalapaaking et al.\ \cite{kalapaaking2022blockchain} proposed a blockchain-based RBAC scheme for smart manufacturing environments. Hao et al.\ \cite{hao2023blockchain} introduced a cross-domain autonomous access control mechanism for IoT using smart contracts. BDVS complements contract-level authorisation with server-side signature verification, preventing a requester from obtaining ciphertext by merely asserting another wallet's address.

\section{System Architecture}

\subsection{High-Level Design}
BDVS comprises three co-operating layers: a client application, a relay backend, and an on-chain smart-contract layer. All cryptographic operations---key derivation, encryption, and decryption---execute exclusively within the client layer, on the user's own device. The backend layer is deliberately reduced to a content-blind relay: it authenticates requests, moves ciphertext between the client and decentralised storage, and never has access to plaintext, keys, or initialisation vectors. Neither the backend nor the storage layer holds sufficient information to recover a document's plaintext; this partitioning is the primary mechanism by which the system protects confidentiality, and it is enforced structurally by the absence of any server-side code path through which the key could be obtained.

