\subsection{Repository Organisation}
The reference implementation is organised into four architectural components, each maintained as a distinct directory within the project's version-control repository \cite{bdvsrepo}: an on-chain component holding the smart contract and its deployment scripts; a relay-backend component holding the API and content-relay logic; a client component holding the browser application and its cryptographic pipeline; and a scripts component holding deployment and instrumentation tooling. The repository README documents the mapping between the architectural roles described in this paper and their corresponding modules in the codebase.

\subsection{System Roles}

\textit{Smart-Contract Layer.} The on-chain document registry maintains a mapping from document digests to owner wallet addresses and manages permission state through grant and revoke operations that emit auditable events. It is the sole source of truth for registration and authorisation decisions.

\textit{Relay Backend.} The backend receives already-encrypted uploads and pins the resulting ciphertext to the decentralised storage layer. It performs no cryptographic operations on file content: it never computes a digest over plaintext, never holds a decryption key, and never decrypts a downloaded file before relaying it to the client. Its responsibilities are (i) verifying the signed, timestamped challenge accompanying each request, (ii) checking view authorisation against the smart contract before serving ciphertext, and (iii) persisting only non-sensitive per-document metadata (storage identifier, owner address, filename) in a server-side document store.

\textit{Client Application.} The client application manages wallet connectivity and owns the full cryptographic pipeline: signature-based key derivation, authenticated symmetric encryption and decryption, client-side \texttt{keccak256} digest computation, and post-decrypt digest re-verification. Every authenticated request to the backend carries a proof of wallet ownership alongside the claimed wallet address.

\section{Security and Encryption Model}

\subsection{File-Level Encryption}
Each document is encrypted entirely on the client, prior to any network transmission, using an authenticated symmetric cipher (AES-256-GCM). A fresh initialisation vector is generated per file, and every ciphertext carries an authentication tag that is verified automatically on decrypt: an invalid tag causes decryption to be rejected before the bytes are treated as file content, giving tamper detection at the cipher layer itself rather than only after a full decrypt-and-rehash cycle. The plaintext file is never transmitted to, or held by, the backend at any point in the upload or download path---only the client and the end user ever observe it.

\subsection{Key Management}
The encryption key is derived deterministically, per wallet, by applying a standard password-based key-derivation function to a wallet signature obtained by prompting the connected wallet to sign a fixed, wallet-specific challenge string, combined with a wallet-specific salt. The same wallet can reconstruct the key by reproducing the same signature input; the server, the storage layer, and other observers see only ciphertext. A different wallet that has been granted contract-level viewing permission cannot derive the owner's encryption key from that permission alone, so cross-wallet decryption requires a separate key-wrapping, re-encryption, or out-of-band key-sharing mechanism that is not implemented in the current prototype. The backend's document store is correspondingly reduced to the storage identifier, owner wallet address, and original filename and contains no decryption key material.

\subsection{Two-Stage Authentication and Authorisation}
Download access is enforced through two sequential controls, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:two-layer-authorization}:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textit{Backend authentication.} The relay verifies a signed, timestamped challenge and confirms that the recovered address matches the claimed wallet address. This prevents address spoofing and bounds replay through a bounded validity window.

\item \textit{Smart-contract authorisation.} After authentication succeeds, the backend checks view authorisation against the on-chain registry before relaying ciphertext. The registry maintains the per-document permission mapping, and exposes grant and revoke operations that propagate permissions across versioned documents sharing a root registration.
\end{enumerate}

The requesting wallet address is proven, not merely asserted. This is achieved through a signed-challenge scheme built on the same message-signing standard used natively by Ethereum wallets for off-chain, human-readable message signing (commonly known as EIP-191): the client signs a timestamped challenge with the connected wallet and transmits the claimed wallet address, the resulting signature, and the signing time alongside the request. Conceptually, this gives the backend three architectural guarantees without ever requiring the wallet's private key to leave the client: (i) the signature can only have been produced by the holder of the wallet's private key, so an address cannot be claimed by an attacker who does not control it; (ii) the signature is cheap for the backend to verify against the claimed address using standard public-key recovery, requiring no shared secret or prior registration step; and (iii) because the signed message embeds a timestamp, the backend can bound how long a captured signature remains usable. If the recovered address does not match the claimed address, the request is rejected before any contract-level check is attempted. A bounded replay window (ten minutes in the current prototype) is enforced against the signed timestamp: signatures older than this window, or timestamped in the future beyond reasonable clock skew, are rejected, bounding the exposure of a captured signature to a short interval rather than granting indefinite reuse.

\subsection{Per-Download Integrity Re-Verification}
Beyond access control, the system performs cryptographic integrity verification on every download, reinforced at two layers. The first layer is the authenticated-cipher tag itself: because the backend relay cannot decrypt on the user's behalf, decryption runs on the client, and any ciphertext modification---whether introduced in transit, at rest in storage, or by a misbehaving relay---causes the authentication-tag check to fail before a single byte of the corrupted content is treated as file data. The second layer: after a successful decrypt, the client recomputes \texttt{keccak256} over the recovered plaintext and compares it against the digest embedded in the requested document identifier, which the client obtained from the on-chain registration record. A mismatch is surfaced to the user as a client-side integrity warning. Registration metadata retrieved from the on-chain registry (owner address, timestamp, block number) is attached to the relay response before decryption, and the client combines these with the local digest match to populate a \textit{Hash Verified} attestation panel, giving the user a human-readable, non-repudiable proof of authenticity derived from immutable on-chain event history and a locally re-computed digest rather than a server's unverifiable say-so.

