\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\subsection{MD5}
MD5 produces a 128-bit digest and executes rapidly in software; however, it is categorically unsuitable for any security-critical application. Practical chosen-prefix collision attacks against MD5 have been demonstrated on commodity hardware since 2004 \cite{wang2005break}, enabling an adversary to construct a fraudulent document whose digest matches that of a legitimately registered original. Deployment of MD5 in BDVS would completely invalidate the integrity guarantee that the system is designed to provide.

\subsection{SHA-256}
SHA-256 is computationally secure and is accessible on the EVM via a dedicated precompiled contract, reducing its cost relative to a fully interpreted implementation. Nevertheless, SHA-256 lacks a direct EVM opcode and must be invoked through the precompile call mechanism, incurring overhead that exceeds the native \texttt{keccak256} opcode. Furthermore, the EVM's built-in \texttt{keccak256} hashing function compiles directly to a native EVM opcode (which implements the Ethereum-specific Keccak variant rather than the NIST SHA-3 standard), placing SHA-256 at a structural cost disadvantage within the EVM execution model.

\subsection{SHA-3 (NIST Standard)}
The NIST-standardised SHA-3 shares the Keccak sponge construction with \texttt{keccak256} but employs a different domain-separation padding scheme. Consequently, SHA-3 is not natively supported by any EVM opcode and must be instantiated in interpreted contract bytecode, incurring approximately 600--800 gas per 32-byte block---one to two orders of magnitude higher than the native \texttt{keccak256} opcode. For a system whose trust model depends on on-chain hash verification, this cost differential is operationally prohibitive at any meaningful registration volume.

\subsection{Blake2b}
Blake2b achieves the highest software throughput (${\approx}900$~MB/s) among the evaluated candidates and provides strong security guarantees, but has no EVM-native representation. On-chain use requires a full interpreted-bytecode implementation, producing gas costs comparable to interpreted SHA-3. Given that the dominant hashing workload in BDVS occurs at the on-chain verification stage, the absence of native EVM support disqualifies Blake2b irrespective of its off-chain throughput advantage.

\subsection{Keccak256 (Selected)}
\texttt{Keccak256} is deeply embedded in the Ethereum protocol stack: it underlies address derivation, transaction hash computation, and the native EVM hashing opcode. The built-in \texttt{keccak256()} hashing function compiles directly to this opcode, whose execution cost is 30 gas plus 6 gas per 32-byte input word, excluding memory-expansion costs. This remains substantially cheaper than software-implemented alternatives. Its 256-bit output provides $2^{128}$ collision resistance under the birthday bound, which is computationally infeasible to breach with current or foreseeable technology. The Keccak sponge construction has withstood over a decade of intensive public cryptanalysis \cite{bertoni2013keccak}.

Three properties make \texttt{keccak256} uniquely appropriate for BDVS: (i) EVM-native gas efficiency (30 gas plus 6 gas per 32-byte word, versus the higher call and execution costs of alternatives); (ii) protocol consistency with the Ethereum toolchain, requiring no additional hashing libraries; and (iii) the absence of any practical collision or preimage attack against its full 256-bit output.

\section{System Design}

\subsection{On-Chain Document Registry}
The on-chain registry stores each document's \texttt{keccak256} digest in a record mapped to the registering wallet address. At an architectural level, the registry exposes the following capabilities:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Register Document} --- records a new digest under the caller's wallet address as its owner; raises a registration event.
\item \textbf{Add Document Version} --- associates a new version digest with an existing root registration; raises a version-added event.
\item \textbf{Grant Viewer Access} / \textbf{Revoke Viewer Access} --- per-document access grant and revocation for a specified wallet.
\item \textbf{Grant Root Viewer Access} / \textbf{Revoke Root Viewer Access} --- root-level access grant and revocation, propagated to all versions associated with a root registration.
\item \textbf{Check View Permission} --- read-only authorisation check invoked by the backend before every download.
\item \textbf{Check Document Exists} --- read-only existence check used by verification operations.
\item \textbf{Revoke Document} / \textbf{Revoke Document Root} --- invalidation capabilities restricted to the document owner.
\end{itemize}

All state-modifying capabilities raise events, establishing an immutable on-chain audit trail. The registry stores the document digest but not the storage identifier, preserving the separation of proof from content.

\subsection{Relay Backend Interface}

The backend exposes a small set of request-handling capabilities. Capabilities that access document-specific resources require the wallet-proof triple described in Section~IV-C (claimed address, signature, and timestamp). Table~\ref{tab:backend-api-endpoints} enumerates them at the level of what each capability does, rather than its concrete route or handler name. The upload pipeline is structured so that all content-sensitive computation happens client-side before the request reaches the backend: the client computes \texttt{keccak256} over the raw plaintext bytes, derives the wallet-specific encryption key, and encrypts the file; only the resulting ciphertext, together with the signed wallet proof, is transmitted to the backend. On the backend, the upload-handling capability buffers the already-encrypted bytes, the signature is verified, and the ciphertext is pinned to decentralised storage; the resulting storage identifier, owner wallet address, and original filename---but no key material---are written atomically to the server-side document store.

