\subsection{Client Application}
The client application manages wallet connectivity and performs the full cryptographic pipeline locally. File registration follows a three-phase protocol: the client first prompts the wallet to sign the encryption-key challenge and derives the encryption key locally; it then computes the \texttt{keccak256} digest over the plaintext and encrypts the file, sending the ciphertext and signed wallet proof to the upload capability; upon receipt of the returned storage identifier, it prompts the wallet a second time to confirm a document-registration transaction carrying the locally computed digest. This ordering guarantees that the file is encrypted client-side and pinned to storage before any on-chain interaction occurs, and that the backend at no point possesses plaintext or key material. If the user declines the registration transaction, the encrypted file persists in storage, but no on-chain digest record exists; the document is therefore not considered registered within the system. On download, the client decrypts the relayed ciphertext locally, re-verifies the \texttt{keccak256} digest against the requested identifier, and surfaces the on-chain proof metadata in a \textit{Hash Verified} panel.

\section{System Workflow}

The upload workflow guides a user through file selection, client-side encryption and registration, and confirmation that the document's digest has been recorded on-chain; the management workflow allows an owner to view their registered documents and to grant or revoke viewer access; and the download workflow surfaces a \textit{Hash Verified} attestation once decryption and digest re-verification succeed on the client. Screenshots of each stage of these workflows, captured from the reference implementation described in Section~\ref{sec:simenv}, are provided in the Appendix (Figs.~\ref{fig:upload-document}--\ref{fig:verified-download}).

