\section{Results and Discussion}

End-to-end testing was conducted on the deployment testnet described in Section~\ref{sec:simenv}. The following outcomes were observed:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textit{Integrity verification.} Modification of any byte in a ciphertext causes authenticated-cipher tag verification to fail client-side, immediately and independently of the on-chain record. Separately, modification of the recovered plaintext (a scenario the authentication tag alone cannot cover, since it only certifies fidelity to what was actually encrypted) alters its \texttt{keccak256} digest, causing the client-side re-verification against the requested identifier to fail. The blockchain record functions as a computationally binding fingerprint at both layers.

\item \textit{File privacy.} Plaintext and decryption keys are not present in any on-chain transaction or backend-persisted record. Storage identifiers are retained by the backend as routing metadata but are not written on-chain. Because encryption, decryption, and key derivation execute client-side, the backend cannot observe plaintext during the document lifecycle; plaintext can be reconstructed only by a client that possesses the corresponding key.

\item \textit{Access control enforcement.} An unauthorised wallet address---even one with knowledge of the document digest and a validly signed request---is rejected when the smart-contract permission check fails. Separately, a request bearing a spoofed wallet address without a matching valid signature is rejected before contract authorisation is attempted. No bypass of either stage was identified during testing.

\item \textit{Transaction costs.} The initial registration call consumed 207,825 gas on the local test node. Grant and revoke operations consumed 56,392 and 27,632 gas respectively, reflecting their different storage-write patterns. At a 25~gwei baseline and \$3,000/ETH, the conservative first-registration estimate corresponds to approximately \$15.59 per document and \$155,869 for 10,000 documents (Table~\ref{tab:volume-cost}).

\item \textit{Two-phase upload correctness.} Declining the registration transaction leaves the encrypted file persisting in storage, but produces no on-chain digest record. The document is therefore not reachable through any authenticated download path. No orphaned on-chain digests without corresponding stored ciphertext were observed during testing.

\item \textit{Client-side cryptographic overhead.} The pipeline's authenticated encryption adds 1.17~ms relative to a direct-hashing baseline on a 1~MB payload (Table~\ref{tab:pipeline-overhead})---negligible against the millisecond-to-second costs of contract reads, storage-gateway fetches, and blockchain confirmation that dominate end-to-end latency (Table~\ref{tab:network-baselines}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Simulation Environment}
\label{sec:simenv}

The architectural roles described in Sections~III--VI are, in this evaluation, realised as a concrete prototype. This section is the sole location in the paper where specific technology choices are named; every other section describes the system purely in terms of architectural roles and capabilities, independent of implementation.

The on-chain document registry was authored in Solidity, compiled and deployed via Hardhat to the Ethereum Sepolia testnet, and accessed from the client through Ethers.js v6. The relay backend was implemented as a Node.js/Express application using ES Modules, receiving already-encrypted multipart uploads via Multer with in-memory buffering, and pinning ciphertext to IPFS through the Pinata REST API; per-document metadata was persisted in a server-side JSON document store. The client application was implemented as a React/TypeScript single-page application built with Vite, communicating with the MetaMask browser extension via the injected \texttt{window.ethereum} provider. All client-side cryptographic operations---signature-based key derivation, AES-256-GCM encryption and decryption, and \texttt{keccak256} digest computation---were performed using the browser's native Web Crypto API (\texttt{crypto.subtle}). The encryption key was derived via PBKDF2 with 100,000 SHA-256 iterations over an EIP-191-signed wallet challenge. Backend API calls were routed through a typed client module that attached the wallet-proof headers (claimed address, signature, and timestamp) to every authenticated request.

Gas and latency measurements reported in Section~VIII were collected against this stack: gas figures were measured on a local Hardhat test node, and latency figures reflect a combination of local browser benchmarks and live calls to Sepolia and the Pinata IPFS gateway. The complete source code for this environment, including the smart contract, backend, and frontend, is available at the project repository \cite{bdvsrepo}, whose README maps each architectural capability named in this paper to its corresponding function in the codebase.

\section{Limitations and Future Scope}

The current implementation is a functional prototype and exhibits several limitations that must be addressed before production deployment. The server-side document store does not support concurrent write operations and will not scale beyond a single-process backend; replacement with a proper database engine would enable concurrent access, indexed queries, and a tamper-evident audit log. Document ownership is bound to a single wallet address; enterprise use cases require ownership transfer primitives or $m$-of-$n$ multi-signature ownership schemes \cite{shamir1979share}. The backend presently performs no request rate limiting, structured logging, or health telemetry, leaving it susceptible to denial-of-service under public deployment conditions.

Client-side key derivation and encryption introduce several open problems that the current prototype does not fully resolve. First, any party that obtains the fixed-message wallet signature can derive the wallet's encryption key; a compromised wallet or phishing-obtained signature therefore compromises every document encrypted under that wallet-derived key. The key-derivation function cannot mitigate disclosure of the signature itself because the signature is the effective keying secret. Second, reliable recovery assumes that the wallet reproduces compatible signature bytes for the fixed challenge. Although common Ethereum wallets use deterministic signing, the design should not depend on wallet-specific signature behaviour without an explicit interoperability and recovery test. Third, contract-level viewer permission does not provide a different wallet with the owner's encryption key. The current grant/revoke interface therefore controls ciphertext retrieval but requires a separate key-wrapping, re-encryption, or secure key-sharing mechanism before an authorised viewer can decrypt the file. Fourth, revocation cannot remove plaintext that a viewer has already downloaded. Fifth, repeated wallet prompts introduce user-experience friction, and no session-caching mechanism for verified signatures is implemented. Finally, hashing and encryption run synchronously on the client's main thread; large documents may therefore block the interface on lower-powered devices unless these operations are moved to a background worker thread.

\section{Conclusion}

BDVS demonstrates a viable architecture for tamper-evident, privacy-preserving document verification using public blockchain infrastructure. Client-side key derivation and authenticated encryption, combined with signed, replay-protected request authentication, mean the backend relay has no code path through which it can reconstruct plaintext from the data it stores.

The selection of \texttt{keccak256} over MD5, SHA-256, SHA-3, and Blake2b is justified by its native EVM execution path, consistency with the Ethereum toolchain, and strong collision resistance. Empirical measurements indicate that upload latency is dominated by blockchain confirmation time (12--36~s) and download latency by storage-gateway cold-cache retrieval (${\approx}5~$s); the isolated microbenchmark measured only 1.17~ms of incremental authenticated-encryption overhead relative to direct hashing. Initial document registration consumed 207,825 gas on the local test node, while measured access-control operations consumed approximately 27,000--57,000 gas. Using the first-registration measurement conservatively, cumulative registration cost reaches an estimated \$155,869 for 10,000 documents at a 25~gwei, \$3,000/ETH baseline.

The combination of client-side encrypted storage, on-chain digest anchoring, signed and replay-protected authentication, smart-contract authorisation, and two-stage cryptographic verification (authenticated-cipher tags plus on-chain digest matching) provides tamper evidence without exposing plaintext to the backend. Signature-derived key recovery, secure key distribution to authorised viewers, and post-download revocation remain acknowledged limitations for future work. A complete reference implementation is available at \cite{bdvsrepo}.

